      '## 1. Peripheral Molecular Clocks in Cutaneous Tissue',
      'The human body does not operate as a static biochemical vessel; it functions as a highly rhythmic, dynamically scheduled biological machine synchronized by the 24-hour solar cycle. While the master circadian pacemaker resides in the suprachiasmatic nucleus (SCN) of the hypothalamus, landmark discoveries in chronobiology have revealed that virtually every peripheral organ—including human skin—possesses its own autonomous molecular clocks.',
